gent case, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $L{{e}^{t/2}}=\\frac{2}{2s-1},$ \ndefined for\n{{field-5}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063724/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b10a18ec998e7fc884cae8fac7b0976beeffb7ebdd5bc53b5668a695168e2e2"} {"question" "q-340732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{t/2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st+t/2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/2-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne \\frac{1}{2}$ or $s=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- If $s\\ne \\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/2-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{1/2-s}{e}^{(1/2-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1/2-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/2}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/2}-\\frac{1}{s-1/2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=\\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{1}{2}) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/2-1/2)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s>\\frac{1}{2}$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>\\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-1/2}-\\frac{1}{s-1/2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/2}-\\frac{1}{s-1/2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{t/2}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{2}{2s-1},$ defined for $s>\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063724/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b10a18ec998e7fc884cae8fac7b0976beeffb7ebdd5bc53b5668a695168e2e2"} {"question" "q-340732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063724/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "924d4809eff1081fd8ed2f5b1c52ce77496c49bc854d73f890bd46f8a7fca3f8"} {"question" "q-340732" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063724/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b10a18ec998e7fc884cae8fac7b0976beeffb7ebdd5bc53b5668a695168e2e2"} {"question" "q-340732" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/2+s)t}\\,\\text{