eference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063724/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b10a18ec998e7fc884cae8fac7b0976beeffb7ebdd5bc53b5668a695168e2e2"} {"question" "q-340732" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063724/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b10a18ec998e7fc884cae8fac7b0976beeffb7ebdd5bc53b5668a695168e2e2"} {"question" "q-340732" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "s>0"] ["math" "s>\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "s\\ge 0"] ["math" "s\\ge \\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "s\\in R"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063724/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b10a18ec998e7fc884cae8fac7b0976beeffb7ebdd5bc53b5668a695168e2e2"} {"question" "q-340732" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "s>\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063724/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b10a18ec998e7fc884cae8fac7b0976beeffb7ebdd5bc53b5668a695168e2e2"} {"question" "q-340733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Complete the arguments in the reasoning below to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-t/4}.$First, we apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=F(s)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nWe now examine the convergence of $F(s)$ for different values of $s:$\n\n$\\,$ For {{field-2}}\nthe integral is divergent.\n\n$\\,$ For {{field-3}}\nthe integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating our integral in the convergent case, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $L{{e}^{-t/4}}=\\frac{4}{4s+1},$ \ndefined for\n{{field-5}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063724/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b10a18ec998e7fc884cae8fac7b0976beeffb7ebdd5bc53b5668a695168e2e2"} {"question" "q-340733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{-t/4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-t/4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st-t/4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne -\\frac{1}{4}$ or $s=-\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- If $s\\ne -\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-1/4-s}{e}^{(-1/4-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1/4-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4}-\\frac{1}{s+1/4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>-\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=-\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-\\frac{1}{4}) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1/4-(-1/4))t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s\\le -\\frac{1}{4}$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s>-\\frac{1}{4}$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>-\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4}-\\frac{1}{s+1/4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4}-\\frac{1}{s+1/4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4s+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-t/4}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{4}{4s+1},$ defined for $s>-\\frac{1}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063724/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b10a18ec998e7fc884cae8fac7b0976beeffb7ebdd5bc53b5668a695168e2e2"} {"question" "q-340733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063724/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "924d4809eff1081fd8ed2f5b1c52ce77496c49bc854d73f890bd46f8a7fca3f8"} {"question" "q-340733" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063724/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b10a18ec998e7fc884cae8fac7b0976beeffb7ebdd5bc53b5668a695168e2e2"} {"question" "q-340733" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1/4+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1/4+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1/4-s)t}\